# Beat 9 of grpconf/synopsis.md — 2:00, the log, named and half-read.
#
# Payload:  grpconf/stage/boblog, 20 243 bytes, four entries.
# Database: grpconf/stage/bobapp1.desc — 64 816 B, 41 files, recovered
#           from bobapp1 sixty seconds earlier.
#
# Invocation:
#   protolens --descriptor-set $DESC1 -I $SRC1 \
#       --script grpconf/beats/log-v1.script $BOBLOG
#
# The document opens NAMED: bobapp.v1.Log at +19, 24 matched, and
# truncated: true.  That last word is the beat.  Draft 2 opened this
# file with no type at all; spec 0314 retired that premise, and the
# honest partial answer is a better one than the veto ever was.
#
# NO OVERRIDES.  Beat 9 resolves nothing.  The Places halves stay junk
# and the beat ends on the `method:` line that names the schema the
# database is missing.  That cliffhanger is what buys beat 10.
#
# Positional paths in this document (the root type is known here, so
# these are stable):
#   /1 /2   Places entries      /3 /4   Routes entries; /4 is the cut one
#   :1 at   :2 method   :3 note   :4 request   :5 response
#
# node: search strings — see infer.script's header for why a search is
# preferred over a path.  Paths are used below only where the beat is
# about *which* entry, which is exactly when a search would be
# ambiguous between four near-identical ones.
#
# Re-pin (must print nothing):
#   protolens --descriptor-set $DESC1 -I $SRC1 \
#       --script grpconf/beats/log-v1.script $BOBLOG script \
#     | grep 'error:'

title: The log, named and half-read

steps:
  - text: |
      A different shape of document, and the first honest one.

      It opened named — bobapp.v1.Log, score 19, twenty-four matched
      fields.  Read the last item in the breakdown out loud:

          truncated: true

      The end of this file is missing.  The process was killed 1 024
      bytes into a record.  The tool did not throw the answer away over
      it, and it did not pretend the file was whole either.  It named
      the type, counted what it could, and reported the damage as a
      property of the answer.

      Press space, then -> to step.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/]
    node: /

  - text: |
      And the damage is localized, in place, in the document.

      Entry 4 carries its own reason:

          entry {  #@ repeated Entry = 1; TRUNCATED_MESSAGE; MISSING: 1024

      The tool does not merely fail — it says how far short the file
      falls.  The length prefix is what makes 1 024 knowable, and it is
      why the damage stops at one entry out of four.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/]
    node: /4

  - text: |
      Now the split this beat exists for.  Entries 3 and 4 are Routes,
      and they open in the clear.

      The request is 84 bytes — the same bytes as beat 8's capture, and
      worth saying so.  ComputeRoutesRequest at -16.  The reply beside
      it is 9 868 bytes at +651, which is what an easy answer looks
      like.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/3]
    node: /3/4

  - text: |
      Entries 1 and 2 are junk.

      This request scores -26 against GeneratedCodeInfo.Annotation.
      The reply below it is a three-way tie at -50.  The cue beside
      them is lit, but dim: the tool is saying there is a message in
      here and I do not have it.

      Leave it hanging.  Do not resolve it in this beat.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/1]
    node: /1/4

  - text: |
      Read the line above the junk.

          method: "/google.maps.places.v1.Places/SearchText"

      The document names the schema it is missing.  And we have already
      seen where that schema is: it was in beat 5's diff, in the second
      binary, ten minutes ago.

      :quit, and we open the same file against the newer build.
    fold: all
    unfold: [/1]
    node: Places/SearchText
